#: ../../../LFI.md:98
msgid "Compiler Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:100
msgid ""
"The LFI target has several configuration options, specified via `-mattr=`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:102
msgid ""
"`+no-lfi-loads`: Disable sandboxing for load instructions (stores-only mode)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:103
msgid "`+no-lfi-stores`: Disable sandboxing for store instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:105
msgid ""
"Use `+no-lfi-loads` to create a \"stores-only\" sandbox that may read, but "
"not write, outside the sandbox region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:108
msgid ""
"Use `+no-lfi-loads,+no-lfi-stores` to create a \"jumps-only\" sandbox that "
"may read/write outside the sandbox region but may not transfer control "
"outside (e.g., may not execute system calls directly). This is primarily "
"useful in combination with some other form of memory sandboxing, such as "
"Intel MPK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:113
msgid "AArch64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:115
msgid ""
"The AArch64 LFI target is `aarch64_lfi`. This is the first part of a target "
"triple that can be used with `--triple=aarch64_lfi-<rest of triple>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:118 ../../../LFI.md:598
msgid "Reserved Registers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:120
msgid ""
"The AArch64 LFI target uses a custom ABI that reserves additional registers "
"for the platform. The registers are listed below, along with the security "
"invariant that must be maintained."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:124
msgid ""
"`x27`: always holds the sandbox base address (must be aligned to the size of "
"the sandbox)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:126
msgid "`x28`: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:127
msgid "`sp`: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:128
msgid "`x30`: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:129
msgid "`x26`: scratch register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:130
msgid "`x25`: context register (see [Context Register](#context-register))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:132
msgid ""
"The current design only supports 4GiB sandboxes, which requires the sandbox "
"base address to be 4GiB-aligned. This is because LFI's ABI stores pointers "
"as their full 64-bit values, rather than just 32-bit offsets from the base. "
"This enables stores-only mode, where loads are not sandboxed but stores are, "
"and allows the host to directly pass pointers to the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:138 ../../../LFI.md:634
msgid "Assembly Rewrites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:140 ../../../LFI.md:636
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:142 ../../../LFI.md:638
msgid "In the following assembly rewrites, some shorthand is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:144
msgid "`xN` or `wN`: refers to any general-purpose non-reserved register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:145 ../../../LFI.md:641
msgid "`{a,b,c}`: matches any of `a`, `b`, or `c`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:146
msgid ""
"`LDSTr`: a load/store instruction that supports register-register addressing "
"modes, with one source/destination register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:147
msgid ""
"`LDSTx`: a load/store instruction not matched by `LDSTr`. This covers load/"
"store pairs (`ldp`/`stp`), SIMD load/stores (`ld1`, `st1`, ...), atomics, "
"exclusives, load/store-release, and unscaled (`ldur`/`stur`) forms. These "
"instructions have a more limited set of addressing modes than `LDSTr`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:149 ../../../LFI.md:643
msgid "Control flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:151
msgid ""
"Indirect branches get rewritten to branch through register `x28`, which must "
"always contain an address within the sandbox. An `add` is used to safely "
"update `x28` with the destination address. Since `ret` uses `x30` by "
"default, which already must contain an address within the sandbox, it does "
"not require any rewrite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:160 ../../../LFI.md:187 ../../../LFI.md:278
#: ../../../LFI.md:308 ../../../LFI.md:353 ../../../LFI.md:378
#: ../../../LFI.md:406 ../../../LFI.md:448 ../../../LFI.md:470
#: ../../../LFI.md:489 ../../../LFI.md:520 ../../../LFI.md:545
#: ../../../LFI.md:569 ../../../LFI.md:670 ../../../LFI.md:693
msgid "Original"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:161 ../../../LFI.md:188 ../../../LFI.md:279
#: ../../../LFI.md:309 ../../../LFI.md:354 ../../../LFI.md:379
#: ../../../LFI.md:407 ../../../LFI.md:449 ../../../LFI.md:471
#: ../../../LFI.md:490 ../../../LFI.md:521 ../../../LFI.md:546
#: ../../../LFI.md:570 ../../../LFI.md:671 ../../../LFI.md:694
msgid "Rewritten"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:177 ../../../LFI.md:647
msgid "Memory accesses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:179
msgid ""
"Memory accesses are rewritten to use the `[x27, wM, uxtw]` addressing mode "
"if it is available, which is automatically safe. Otherwise, rewrites fall "
"back to using `x28` along with an instruction to safely load it with the "
"target address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:270
msgid "Stack pointer modification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:272
msgid ""
"When the stack pointer is modified, we write the modified value to a "
"temporary, before moving it back into `sp` with a safe `add`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:295
msgid "Link register modification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:297
msgid ""
"When the link register is modified, it is guarded back into the sandbox with "
"a safe `add x30, x27, w30, uxtw`. This guard is deferred until the next "
"control-flow instruction rather than emitted immediately after the "
"modification. Deferral keeps a signed return address intact so that a "
"following authentication instruction (such as `autiasp`) can run before the "
"guard, which would otherwise destroy the pointer authentication signature. "
"See [Pointer Authentication Code (PAC) support](#pointer-authentication-code-"
"pac-support)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:330
msgid "Pointer Authentication Code (PAC) support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:332
msgid ""
"LFI is compatible with Arm Pointer Authentication Code (PAC) instructions, "
"which are used to sign and authenticate `x30` to protect against control-"
"flow hijacking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:336
msgid ""
"The typical use is `-mbranch-protection=pac-ret`, which signs only the "
"return address in `x30` using the hint-space `paciasp` and `autiasp` "
"instructions. The combined authenticate-and-branch and authenticate-and-"
"return instructions covered below require Armv8.3-a and are not produced by "
"`-mbranch-protection`. They can appear in hand-written assembly or from "
"environments that sign all code pointers, so the rewriter still sandboxes "
"them rather than passing them through unmodified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:344
msgid ""
"To gain the security benefit of PAC under LFI, the hardware must implement "
"`FEAT_FPAC`, so that authentication failures fault immediately. Without "
"`FEAT_FPAC`, a failed authentication produces a poisoned pointer, which LFI "
"still keeps confined to the sandbox by masking it, but the mask overwrites "
"the poison caused by the authentication failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:372
msgid ""
"Authenticated returns (`retaa`/`retab`) combine authentication with return, "
"and must be expanded during rewriting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:398
msgid ""
"Authenticated branches (`braa`/`brab`/`braaz`/`brabz`) and calls (`blraa`/"
"`blrab`/`blraaz`/`blrabz`) combine authentication with an indirect branch or "
"call. They are expanded by first authenticating the target register in "
"place, then performing a normal sandboxed branch or call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:434
msgid ""
"Authenticated exception returns (`eret`/`eretaa`/`eretab`) are privileged "
"and are not supported: the rewriter reports an error for them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:437 ../../../LFI.md:659
msgid "System instructions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:439
msgid ""
"System calls are rewritten into a sequence that loads the address of the "
"first runtime call entrypoint and jumps to it. The runtime call entrypoint "
"table is stored at a negative offset from the sandbox base, so it can be "
"referenced by `x27`. The rewrite also saves and restores the link register, "
"since it is used for branching into the runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:461
msgid "Thread pointer (TP)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:463
msgid ""
"TP accesses are rewritten into loads/stores from the context register "
"(`x25`), which holds the virtual thread pointer at offset 16 (see [Context "
"Register](#context-register))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:472
msgid "`mrs xN, tpidr_el0`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:473
msgid "`ldr xN, [x25, #16]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:474
msgid "`msr tpidr_el0, xN`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:475
msgid "`str xN, [x25, #16]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:478
msgid "Optimizations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:480
msgid "Basic guard elimination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:482
msgid ""
"If a register is guarded multiple times in the same basic block without any "
"modifications to it during the intervening instructions, then subsequent "
"guards can be removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:507
msgid "Address generation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:509 ../../../LFI.md:534 ../../../LFI.md:562
msgid "**Note**: not yet implemented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:511
msgid ""
"Addresses to global symbols in position-independent executables are "
"frequently generated via `adrp` followed by `ldr`. Since the address "
"generated by `adrp` can be statically guaranteed to be within the sandbox, "
"it is safe to directly target `x28` for these sequences. This allows the "
"omission of a guard instruction before the `ldr`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:532
msgid "Stack guard elimination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:536
msgid ""
"If the stack pointer is modified by adding/subtracting a small immediate, "
"and then later used to perform a memory access without any intervening "
"jumps, then the guard on the stack pointer modification can be removed. This "
"is because the load/store is guaranteed to trap if the stack pointer has "
"been moved outside of the sandbox region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:560
msgid "Guard hoisting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:564
msgid "In certain cases, guards may be hoisted outside of loops."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:594
msgid "X86-64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:596
msgid "The X86-64 LFI target is `x86_64_lfi`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:600
msgid "The X86-64 LFI target reserves the following registers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:602
msgid ""
"`r14`: always holds the sandbox base address. Also used as the runtime call "
"table pointer (the runtime call table is stored at the sandbox base)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:604
msgid ""
"`gs`: always holds the sandbox base address (used as a segment register for "
"memory access sandboxing)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:606
msgid "`rsp`: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:607
msgid "`r15`: context register (see [Context Register](#context-register))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:608
msgid "`r11`: scratch register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:610
msgid "Bundling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:612
msgid ""
"The X86-64 LFI target confines control flow using 32-byte bundles. Indirect "
"branch targets are masked to a 32-byte boundary, so control can only enter "
"code at a bundle-aligned address. For this to constrain the instruction "
"stream, no instruction may span a bundle boundary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:617
msgid ""
"LFI object files are therefore assembled with {doc}`aligned instruction "
"bundling <AlignedBundling>` enabled, which inserts `nop` padding wherever an "
"instruction would otherwise cross a boundary. Bundling is enabled implicitly "
"for the `x86_64_lfi` target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:622
msgid ""
"A rewrite that expands one instruction into a sequence which must not be "
"entered in the middle wraps that sequence in `.bundle_lock` / `."
"bundle_unlock`. This keeps the whole group within a single bundle, so no "
"masked indirect branch can land between its instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:627
msgid ""
"**Note**: the masking of indirect branch targets is part of the control flow "
"rewrites, which have not been implemented yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:630
msgid ""
"Bundling is specific to X86-64. AArch64 instructions are fixed-width and "
"naturally aligned, and the AArch64 LFI target confines indirect branches by "
"guarding the target register instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:640
msgid "`%rN` or `%eN`: refers to any general-purpose non-reserved register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:645 ../../../LFI.md:649 ../../../LFI.md:653
#: ../../../LFI.md:657
msgid "**Note**: these rewrites have not been implemented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:651
msgid "String instructions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:655
msgid "Stack modification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:661
msgid ""
"System calls are rewritten into a sequence that loads the return address "
"into the scratch register and jumps to the runtime call handler. The runtime "
"call handler table is stored at the address pointed to by `r14`. The `r11` "
"register stores the return address (marked by the label `.Ltmp` in the block "
"below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:682
msgid "Thread pointer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.md:684
msgid ""
"Thread pointer accesses via the `%fs` segment (used for TLS) are rewritten "
"to use the virtual thread pointer from the context register (`r15`) at "
"offset 16 (see [Context Register](#context-register)). The rewrite handles "
"any load or store instruction with an `%fs`-segment memory operand. `Op` "
"represents any such instruction."
msgstr ""

